const body = `<h1>Student RPG Guide: Character Creator</h1>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>This is YOUR guide.</strong> In Unit 1 you built the dice engine. Now you
    build a character on top of it: six ability scores, hit points, armor class, and a
    character sheet. Instead of code blanks, you are provided with <strong>Acceptance
    Criteria</strong> — a downloadable test that runs your code and tells you whether
    it is correct.
</div>

<p>
    The rules are <strong>Dungeons &amp; Dragons 5th edition</strong>, simplified where
    full 5e would need tools you have not met yet. Every formula below is the real 5e
    one, or a deliberate trimming of it. Where a rule is trimmed, this guide says so.
</p>

<h2>The Setup</h2>

<p>Three files. You write the first one:</p>

<ol>
    <li><code>Character.java</code> — the character itself. This is today's build.</li>
    <li><code>Dice.java</code> — your engine from Unit 1.7. <code>generateStats()</code>
        and <code>abilityCheck()</code> both call it, so it must sit in the same folder.</li>
    <li><a href="../../data/CharacterCheck.java" download>CharacterCheck.java</a> — the
        acceptance test. You do not write this one. Download it.</li>
</ol>

<p>
    You will also want a <code>CharacterTester.java</code> with a <code>main</code>
    method, for experiments of your own. That file is yours to play in — nothing tests it.
</p>

<p>Start <code>Character.java</code> with the class outline and its fields:</p>

<pre><code>public class Character {

    public String name = "Unnamed";
    public int level = 1;

    public int strength, dexterity, constitution;
    public int intelligence, wisdom, charisma;

    public int hp, maxHp, ac, initiative;
    public int proficiencyBonus, passivePerception;
}</code></pre>

<p>
    The <code>= "Unnamed"</code> and <code>= 1</code> are <strong>field
    initializers</strong> — the value a field holds before anything is assigned to it.
    Without them, a new <code>String</code> field is <code>null</code> and your character
    sheet prints <code>null</code> for a name.
</p>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>The fields are <code>public</code> in this unit, and that is deliberate.</strong>
    In a finished class, fields are <code>private</code> and the outside world reaches
    them through getters. You will do exactly that in Unit 5 — to this file. For now
    public fields keep the ceremony out of your way while you learn what an object
    <em>is</em>, and they cost you nothing you can measure yet.
    <br><br>
    They do cost you something, though, and you will feel it twice:
    <br>
    <strong>1.</strong> Your field <em>names</em> are part of the contract.
    <code>CharacterCheck</code> reads <code>hero.strength</code> and
    <code>hero.maxHp</code> directly, so renaming <code>hp</code> to
    <code>hitPoints</code> stops the test compiling.
    <br>
    <strong>2.</strong> Nothing stops you writing <code>hero.level = 9</code> and leaving
    <code>proficiencyBonus</code> saying <code>+2</code> — a sheet that contradicts
    itself. There is no method that changes one value and repairs its consequences. Keep
    both of those in mind; they are the whole agenda for Unit 5.
</div>

<hr>

<h2>How to Run the Acceptance Test</h2>

<p>
    <code>CharacterCheck.java</code> calls every method in this guide and prints
    <code>PASS</code> or <code>FAIL</code> for each one. It does not read your code —
    it <em>runs</em> it, so the only way to pass is to actually be correct.
</p>

<ol>
    <li>
        Download <a href="../../data/CharacterCheck.java" download>CharacterCheck.java</a>
        and put it in the same folder as your <code>Character.java</code> and
        <code>Dice.java</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Open a terminal in that folder.</li>
    <li>
        Compile all three files together:
        <pre><code>javac Dice.java Character.java CharacterCheck.java</code></pre>
        All three must compile before anything runs. If the error message names one of
        your methods or fields, that name is missing or its shape does not match this
        guide — fix your <code>Character.java</code>, not the test file.
    </li>
    <li>
        Run the tests:
        <pre><code>java CharacterCheck</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li>
        Read the output. Every check is a <code>PASS</code> or a <code>FAIL</code>,
        and the last two lines count them:
        <pre><code>44 passed, 0 failed
Your Character class meets the spec.</code></pre>
    </li>
</ol>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>If the compile step fails on everything at once</strong>, check the file
    name before you check the logic. <code>javac</code> requires the class inside
    <code>Character.java</code> to be named <code>Character</code>. A file called
    <code>Character.java</code> holding <code>public class Player</code> will not compile,
    no matter how good the code inside is.
</div>

<p>
    <strong>Why ranges, and never exact numbers?</strong> <code>generateStats()</code>
    rolls real dice, so a correct <code>strength</code> is allowed to be anything from
    3 to 18. The test cannot demand a particular score. Instead it generates hundreds of
    characters and checks two things: that every score lands <em>in range</em>, and that
    your derived values are <em>correctly related</em> to the scores — it compares your
    <code>maxHp</code> against <code>10 + modifier(constitution)</code>, using your own
    modifier method. That works no matter what the dice rolled, and it still catches a
    wrong formula.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>The 5e Rules This Class Uses</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Value</th><th>5e rule</th><th>Formula</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Ability modifier</td>
        <td>(score − 10) ÷ 2, rounded down</td>
        <td><code>(score - 10) / 2</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Hit points at level 1</td>
        <td>hit die maximum + CON modifier</td>
        <td><code>10 + modifier(constitution)</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Armor class, unarmored</td>
        <td>10 + DEX modifier</td>
        <td><code>10 + modifier(dexterity)</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Initiative</td>
        <td>DEX modifier</td>
        <td><code>modifier(dexterity)</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Passive Perception</td>
        <td>10 + WIS modifier</td>
        <td><code>10 + modifier(wisdom)</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Proficiency bonus</td>
        <td>+2 at level 1, rising every four levels</td>
        <td><code>2 + (level - 1) / 4</code></td>
    </tr>
</table>

<p>
    <strong>Why a 10 for hit points?</strong> In 5e your starting HP is your class's hit
    die maximum plus your CON modifier. A Fighter's hit die is a d10, so level 1 gives
    10 + CON. We use the Fighter's d10 for every character — one fewer rule to track,
    same shape.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Where we trimmed 5e.</strong> A real skill check adds your proficiency bonus
    when you are proficient in the skill. <code>abilityCheck()</code> leaves that out, so
    it can hand the work straight to your Unit 1 engine. The proficiency bonus is still
    computed and displayed — it just does not enter the check.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 1: modifier(int score) — Score to Modifier</h2>

<p>
    This is the 5e rule underneath everything else. An ability score of 14 is not a +14
    to anything; it is a +2. Learn this one method and every derived value below becomes
    a single line.
</p>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public int modifier(int score)</code></li>
    <li>Returns <code>(score - 10) / 2</code></li>
    <li>Works for scores above <em>and</em> below 10 — a score of 8 gives −1, a score of 6 gives −2</li>
    <li>Do not round up. A score of 11 is a +0 in 5e, not a +1</li>
</ul>

<h3>Socratic Checkpoint:</h3>
<p>
    Why subtract 10? Because 10 is the score of an average person, so 10 must come out to
    +0 — no bonus, no penalty. Why divide by 2? Because 5e wants each +1 to cost two
    points of score. Work out the modifier for 10, 12, and 20 before you write the code.
</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>System.out.println("14 -> " + c.modifier(14));  // +2
System.out.println("10 -> " + c.modifier(10));  // +0
System.out.println(" 8 -> " + c.modifier(8));   // -1</code></pre>
<p>
    Run it. Is 18 a +4? Is 4 a −3? If 14 comes back as 14, you forgot to do the
    arithmetic. If 18 comes back as 8, you forgot to divide.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 2: generateStats() — Roll the Six Abilities</h2>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public void generateStats()</code></li>
    <li>Rolls <strong>six</strong> ability scores using <code>Dice.statRoll()</code> from Unit 1</li>
    <li>
        Stores them in this order, which is the order <code>Dice.statRoll()</code>
        returns them in:
        <code>strength, dexterity, constitution, intelligence, wisdom, charisma</code>
    </li>
    <li>Each stat is a 3d6 roll, so each lands between 3 and 18</li>
    <li>Then fills in every derived value from the table above, and sets <code>hp</code> to <code>maxHp</code></li>
    <li>Leaves <code>name</code> and <code>level</code> alone — those are not rolled</li>
</ul>

<h3>Socratic Checkpoint:</h3>
<p>
    <code>Dice.statRoll()</code> returns an <code>int[]</code> of six values. What is the
    index of the first one? The last one? If you wrote the array's values into the six
    fields in the wrong order, would <code>CharacterCheck</code> notice? Think carefully —
    the dice are rolled inside <code>Dice</code>, so the test never sees which number was
    meant for which ability. This is a check you have to make yourself.
</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>Character c = new Character();
c.generateStats();
System.out.println("STR " + c.strength + "  DEX " + c.dexterity);
System.out.println("CON " + c.constitution + "  HP " + c.maxHp);</code></pre>
<p>
    Run it several times. Every stat should move around between 3 and 18. If all six
    stats are always identical, you are filling them from one roll instead of six.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 3: proficiencyBonus — The 5e Level Table</h2>

<p>
    In 5e your proficiency bonus is not something you pick; it is a function of your
    level. It is +2 for levels 1 through 4, then rises by one every four levels, topping
    out at +6.
</p>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li><code>level</code> starts at <strong>1</strong> (that is the field initializer)</li>
    <li><code>generateStats()</code> sets <code>proficiencyBonus</code> to <code>2 + (level - 1) / 4</code></li>
</ul>

<table>
    <tr><th>Level</th><td>1–4</td><td>5–8</td><td>9–12</td><td>13–16</td><td>17–20</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Bonus</th><td>+2</td><td>+3</td><td>+4</td><td>+5</td><td>+6</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Socratic Checkpoint:</h3>
<p>
    Check the formula against the table. At level 4 you get <code>2 + 3 / 4</code>, which
    is <code>2 + 0</code>. At level 5 you get <code>2 + 4 / 4</code>, which is
    <code>2 + 1</code>. Why does integer division make this formula work — where would it
    break if Java rounded instead of truncating?
</p>

<p>
    <strong>There is something wrong here, and you should notice it.</strong> Your
    proficiency bonus — how good you are at the things you are trained in — is computed
    inside <code>generateStats()</code>, whose job is rolling ability scores. Rolling dice
    has nothing to do with how proficient you are. And because that is the only method
    that recomputes the bonus, levelling up means rerolling your entire character:
</p>
<pre><code>c.level = 9;
c.printCharacterSheet();   // says Level 9 and Proficiency: +2. A lie.
c.generateStats();         // now +4 - but every stat was rerolled</code></pre>
<p>
    Nothing here is a bug you can fix with the tools you have. It is a design problem,
    and it is the reason Unit 5 exists. You will fix it on this file, properly, in Unit 5.
</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>Character c = new Character();
c.level = 9;
c.generateStats();
System.out.println("Level " + c.level + " -> +" + c.proficiencyBonus);  // +4</code></pre>
<p>
    Try levels 1, 5, 9, and 17. You should see +2, +3, +4, and +6.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 4: abilityCheck(int dc, int score) — Skill Checks</h2>

<p>
    The payoff for Unit 1. An ability check is a d20 roll plus the relevant ability
    modifier, against a difficulty class. You already wrote this: it is
    <code>Dice.skillCheck(pass, skill)</code>.
</p>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public int abilityCheck(int dc, int score)</code></li>
    <li><code>dc</code> is the difficulty to beat; <code>score</code> is the ability score being tested</li>
    <li>Delegates to <code>Dice.skillCheck(dc, score)</code> — do not rewrite the d20 logic</li>
    <li>Returns the same four codes: −1 crit fail, 0 fail, 1 success, 2 crit success</li>
</ul>

<h3>Socratic Checkpoint:</h3>
<p>
    You are calling a method you wrote in Unit 1 rather than copying its body. If you
    later find a bug in <code>skillCheck</code>, how many places would you have to fix —
    and how many if <code>Character</code> had its own copy of the d20 logic?
</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>System.out.println("STR check, DC 14: " + c.abilityCheck(14, c.strength));
System.out.println("INT check, DC 14: " + c.abilityCheck(14, c.intelligence));</code></pre>
<p>
    Run each twenty times. A character with a high strength should succeed on a strength
    check more often than on an intelligence check. If both look the same, check that you
    are passing the score through to <code>Dice.skillCheck</code> and not dropping it.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 5: printCharacterSheet() — Show Your Creation</h2>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public void printCharacterSheet()</code></li>
    <li>Prints the character's name, an HP bar, and all six ability scores</li>
    <li>The HP bar is <strong>exactly 10 characters</strong> wide, between <code>[</code> and <code>]</code>:
        filled blocks first, then empty blocks</li>
    <li>An HP line shows the current and maximum hit points as <code>hp/maxHp</code></li>
    <li>Use <code>System.out.println()</code> to format each line</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Sample output:</strong></p>
<pre><code><span class="output">====================
== GROG THE MIGHTY ==
====================
Level 1   HP: [##########] 12/12

STR: 15
DEX: 10
CON: 14
INT:  8
WIS: 11
CHA:  9

AC: 10
Initiative: 0
Passive Perception: 11
Proficiency: +2
====================</span></code></pre>

<h3>The HP bar is a formula</h3>
<p>
    The bar shows what fraction of your hit points remain, out of 10 blocks. That makes it
    a calculation, not a decoration — and it hides the integer-division trap from Unit 1:
</p>
<pre><code>int filled = hp * 10 / maxHp;     // correct
int filled = hp / maxHp * 10;     // always 0 at half health - division happens first</code></pre>
<p>
    At 50 of 100 hit points, <code>50 / 100</code> is <code>0</code>, so the second line
    gives an empty bar at half health. The first line multiplies before dividing and gives
    the 5 blocks you expect. Work out why, then build the bar:
</p>
<pre><code>String full  = "##########";
String empty = "..........";
String bar = full.substring(0, filled) + empty.substring(0, 10 - filled);</code></pre>
<p>
    <code>substring(0, filled)</code> takes the first <code>filled</code> characters of
    the ten-block string. Two substrings added together always make exactly 10 characters.
    You met <code>substring</code> in the String unit — this is what it is for.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Use plain ASCII for the bar.</strong> Blocks (<code>█</code> and
    <code>░</code>) look better, but they are not ASCII, and on some machines
    <code>javac</code> will misread them and turn your 10-character bar into
    something longer. If you want blocks anyway, compile with
    <code>javac -encoding UTF-8 ...</code>. The test checks the bar's
    <em>width</em>, not which character you drew, so either choice can pass.
</div>

<p>
    <strong>Nothing clamps <code>hp</code>.</strong> In 5e, hit points never fall below 0
    and never rise above your maximum. This class cannot enforce that yet — there is no
    setter, and the field is public, so <code>c.hp = 999;</code> compiles and quietly
    produces a bar wider than ten characters. Enforcing a legal range on the way in is a
    Unit 5 topic, and guarding fields is how you do it.
</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>Character c = new Character();
c.name = "Grog the Mighty";
c.generateStats();
c.printCharacterSheet();</code></pre>
<p>
    Expected: the name, a full bar at full health, and six labeled scores between 3 and
    18. Then try <code>c.hp = c.maxHp / 2;</code> and print again — the bar should be
    about half filled. If it does not move when HP changes, your <code>filled</code>
    calculation is not using <code>hp</code>.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Full Method Summary</h2>

<p>When complete, your class should have exactly this shape:</p>

<pre><code>public class Character {

    public String name = "Unnamed";
    public int level = 1;

    public int strength, dexterity, constitution;
    public int intelligence, wisdom, charisma;

    public int hp, maxHp, ac, initiative;
    public int proficiencyBonus, passivePerception;

    public int  modifier(int score)
    public void generateStats()
    public int  abilityCheck(int dc, int score)
    public void printCharacterSheet()
}</code></pre>

<p>
    Four methods, twelve fields, and no getters — because there is nothing to get
    <em>through</em>. That is the shape of a class before encapsulation, and it is
    entirely workable. It is just not finished.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Check yourself against the test, not against this page.</strong> This guide
    and <code>CharacterCheck.java</code> describe the same contract. If they ever seem to
    disagree, the test wins — it is what will be run.
</div>

<h2>What Unit 5 Will Do To This File</h2>

<p>
    Nothing in this guide is wasted, and nothing in it is <em>wrong</em> — but it has two
    flaws you should be able to name by the time you finish 2.7. You will fix both in
    Unit 5, on this file:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>The fields are naked.</strong> Anything that can reach a
        <code>Character</code> can set <code>hp</code> to 999 or <code>level</code> to 9
        without consequence. Unit 5 makes them <code>private</code> and adds getters, so
        the only way in is through methods you wrote.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>A derived value cannot be updated on its own.</strong>
        <code>proficiencyBonus</code> follows from <code>level</code>, but the only line
        that recomputes it lives inside <code>generateStats()</code> — alongside a full
        reroll of every ability score. Unit 5 adds a setter that changes the <code>level</code>
        and repairs its consequence, and nothing else.
    </li>
</ul>

<p>
    Keep this file. You will hand it back to yourself, with a much better version of it
    beside it.
</p>`;
